To force a text field to display columns using Adobe's JavaScript, you can create a custom cell renderer. This involves using the `TextField` class and overriding its rendering method to display the data in columns.

Here's an example of how to achieve this:

1. Create a new class that extends the `TextField` class.
2. Override the `paint` method to handle the column rendering.
3. Set the custom renderer to the text field.

Below is an example code snippet that demonstrates this:

```javascript
// Custom cell renderer class
class ColumnRenderer extends TextField {
  constructor(data) {
    super();
    this.data = data;
  }

  paint(g) {
    const x = 0;
    const y = 0;
    const rowHeight = 20;
    const columnWidth = 100;
    const maxColumns = 5;

    // Loop through the data and render each value in a column
    for (let i = 0; i < this.data.length; i++) {
      const value = this.data[i];
      const column = Math.floor(i / maxColumns);
      const row = i % maxColumns;

      g.setColor(0x000000); // Set text color
      g.setFont(14); // Set font size
      g.drawString(value, x + column * columnWidth, y + row * rowHeight);
    }
  }
}

// Example usage
const data = ["Apple", "Banana", "Cherry", "Date", "Elderberry", "Fig", "Grape", "Honeydew"];
const columnRenderer = new ColumnRenderer(data);
columnRenderer.setSize(300, 200); // Set size of the text field
columnRenderer.setAutoResize(true); // Enable auto-resize
columnRenderer.setText(new StringColumn(columnRenderer, data)); // Set the custom renderer

// Add the text field to the stage
this.addChild(columnRenderer);
```

In this example, the `ColumnRenderer` class extends `TextField` and overrides the `paint` method to draw each value in a column format. The `data` array contains the values to be displayed, and the `paint` method calculates the position for each value based on its index.

You can adjust the `columnWidth` and `rowHeight` variables to fit your desired column and row sizes, as well as the `maxColumns` variable to control the number of columns displayed.

This code should help you achieve a column-based text field display in Adobe's JavaScript environment.

Absolutely, I'd be happy to help you get started with creating a SwiftUI app! SwiftUI is a powerful and easy-to-use framework for building user interfaces on Apple platforms. Below, I'll guide you through the basics of setting up a simple SwiftUI app and adding some UI components to it.

1. **Setting Up the Project:**
   - Open Xcode and create a new project.
   - Select "App" as the template.
   - Name your project (e.g., "MySwiftUIApp").
   - Choose Swift as the language.
   - Select "SwiftUI" as the interface.
   - Choose "SwiftUI App" as the lifecycle.
   - Click "Create."

2. **Creating the Main View:**
   - Go to the `ContentView.swift` file. This is where we'll define the main view of our app.
   - Replace the content of the `ContentView` struct with the following code:

```swift
import SwiftUI

struct ContentView: View {
    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text("Hello, SwiftUI!")
                .font(.largeTitle)
                .padding()
            Text("Welcome to your first SwiftUI app!")
                .font(.title)
                .padding()
        }
    }
}

struct ContentView_Previews: PreviewProvider {
    static var previews: some View {
        ContentView()
    }
}
```

3. **Running the App:**
   - Select an iOS simulator or a real device from the toolbar and click the "Run" button (the play button) in the top-left corner of Xcode.

You should see a simple app displaying two text views with different fonts and padding.

4. **Adding More UI Components:**
   - Let's add a button that changes the text when tapped.

Modify the `ContentView` struct to include a state variable and a button:

```swift
struct ContentView: View {
    @State private var welcomeMessage = "Welcome to your first SwiftUI app!"

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text(welcomeMessage)
                .font(.largeTitle)
                .padding()
            Button(action: {
                welcomeMessage = "Button tapped!"
            }) {
                Text("Tap me!")
                    .padding()
                    .background(Color.blue)
                    .foregroundColor(.white)
                    .cornerRadius(10)
            }
            .padding()
        }
    }
}
```

5. **Exploring More Components:**
   - You can explore more components like `TextField`, `Picker`, `Slider`, etc., and customize them as needed.

6. **Organizing Code:**
   - As your app grows, consider breaking down your views into smaller, reusable components (e.g., custom buttons, forms).

7. **Previewing Your App:**
   - Xcode provides a great way to preview your app in real-time. You can see how your app looks in different device sizes and orientations.

Feel free to ask any questions or request further assistance as you continue developing your SwiftUI app!

