    if (result == ERROR) {
        printf("An error occurred.\n");
    } else {
        printf("Function executed successfully.\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, `myFunction` returns `SUCCESS` if it completes successfully, or `ERROR` if it encounters an error. The caller of `myFunction` checks the return value and handles the error accordingly. This is a simple and widely used pattern for error handling in C.

